/*!
@defgroup rt2k_pmu PMU: Power Management Unit Driver
@details The PMU driver provides APIs to configure and control the Power Management Unit.

# Overview {#PMUOverview}

The Power Management Unit (PMU) driver provides comprehensive control over:
- Voltage regulators (LDOs) for multiple power rails
- Temperature sensor with configurable thresholds
- Voltage monitoring with per-rail thresholds
- Body bias configuration for power optimization
- Low-frequency oscillator control (LFFRO1M and XOSC32K)
- Interrupt management for power events
- Reset gate control (warm and cold reset)
- FSM (Finite State Machine) bypass control

## Features {#PMUFeatures}

The PMU driver supports:
- **Multiple voltage rails**: VDD_1V5, VDD_1V8, VDDA_1V8, VDD_0V8, VDD_CORE, VDD_PMU
- **Temperature monitoring**: Configurable high (120°C to 135°C) and low (-20°C to -50°C) thresholds
- **Voltage monitoring**: Per-rail upper/lower voltage thresholds with interrupt capability
- **Body bias control**: Zero (ZBB), N-well only, P-well only, or both modes with VDD tracking
- **Power optimization**: LDO bypass modes, voltage trimming (nominal, +5%, +10%, +20%)
- **Clock sources**: 1MHz LFFRO, 32kHz crystal oscillator with self-charge or transconductance modes
- **Interrupt handling**: Comprehensive interrupt support for voltage

# Function groups {#PMUFunctionGroups}

## Initialization and Configuration {#PMUInit}

The PMU driver provides configuration functions for each subsystem:
- PMU_SetFsmBypass() - Configure FSM bypass mode
- PMU_ConfigLffro1M() - Configure 1MHz low-frequency oscillator
- PMU_ConfigTemperatureSensor() - Set temperature monitoring thresholds

## Voltage Regulator Control {#PMURegulators}

Configure individual voltage rails:
- PMU_ConfigVdd1V5() - Configure VDD_1V5 regulator
- PMU_ConfigVdd1V8() - Configure VDD_1V8 regulator  
- PMU_ConfigVdda1V8() - Configure VDDA_1V8 regulator
- PMU_ConfigVdd0V8() - Configure VDD_0V8 regulator
- PMU_ConfigLpLdos() - Configure low-power LDOs
- PMU_BuckVddCoreInit - Configure Buck VDD_Core

## Voltage Monitoring {#PMUMonitoring}

Set voltage monitoring thresholds:
- PMU_ConfigVddCoreSensors() - Configure VDD_CORE voltage thresholds
- PMU_ConfigVddPmuMonitors() - Configure VDD_PMU monitoring thresholds

## Body Bias Control {#PMUBodyBias}

Optimize power through body bias:
- PMU_BodyBiasConfigure() - Set body bias parameters
- PMU_BodyBiasApply() - Apply body bias configuration

## Interrupt Management {#PMUInterrupts}

Handle PMU events:
- PMU_EnableInterrupts() - Enable specific PMU interrupts
- PMU_DisableInterrupts() - Disable specific PMU interrupts
- PMU_ClearInterruptStatusFlags() - Clear interrupt flags
- PMU_GetInterruptStatusFlags() - Read interrupt status
- PMU_GetInterruptStatus() - Gets PMU interrupt status

## Reset Control {#PMUReset}

Control reset behavior:
- PMU_ConfigWarmResetGates() - Configure warm reset gates
- PMU_ConfigColdResetGates() - Configure cold reset gates

## Status Monitoring {#PMUStatus}

Monitor PMU status:
- PMU_GetStatus() - Read PMU status register
- PMU_WaitVddCoreOk() - Wait for VDD_CORE OK status

# Hardware Requirements {#PMUHardwareReq}

The PMU module requires:
- External power supply connections for monitored rails
- Proper decoupling capacitors on power rails
- Temperature sensor calibration for accurate measurements
- Clock source for body bias operation

# Power Domains {#PMUPowerDomains}

The PMU manages multiple power domains:

## Core Power Domain
- VDD_CORE: Main digital core supply (0.8V nominal)
- Supplied by integrated buck converter or external source

## I/O Power Domain  
- VDD_1V8: Digital I/O supply (1.8V nominal)
- VDDA_1V8: Analog I/O supply (1.8V nominal)

## Internal Power Domain
- VDD_0V8: Internal digital supply (0.8V nominal)
- VDD_1V5: Internal reference supply (1.5V nominal)

## PMU Power Domain
- VDD_PMU: Always-on PMU supply (3.3V nominal)
- Powers PMU control logic and monitoring circuits

# Configuration Guidelines {#PMUConfigGuide}

## Voltage Threshold Selection
- Lower thresholds should be set above minimum operating voltage
- Upper thresholds should be set below absolute maximum ratings
- Allow margin for voltage ripple and transients

## Temperature Threshold Selection
- High temperature threshold typically set 10-15°C below junction max
- Low temperature threshold based on minimum operating temperature
- Consider thermal resistance and ambient conditions

## Body Bias Configuration
- Zero body bias (ZBB) for normal operation
- Forward body bias (FBB) for performance boost
- Reverse body bias (RBB) for leakage reduction
- VDD tracking maintains optimal bias across voltage changes

## LDO Trim Settings
- Nominal trim for standard operation
- Plus 5/10/20% trim for voltage adjustment
- Consider load regulation and line regulation effects

# Notes {#PMUNotes}

- Always configure voltage thresholds appropriate for your system requirements
- Body bias configuration should be applied carefully as it affects chip performance
- Temperature thresholds should be set based on operating environment specifications
- Some configurations may require specific power sequencing - consult the reference manual
- W1C (Write-1-to-Clear) registers are handled automatically by the driver
- Interrupt flags must be cleared before re-enabling interrupts
- FSM bypass should only be used for debug or special test modes
*/
